Proteins are important to man because they:

1. are needed for **growth**: they build new cells and tissues (muscles, skin, hair, nails);
2. **repair worn-out and damaged tissues** (healing of wounds);
3. make **enzymes**, which control all chemical reactions of the body;
4. make **hormones** (for example insulin, growth hormone);
5. make **antibodies**, which protect the body against disease (defence and immunity);
6. make **haemoglobin**, which carries oxygen in the blood, and other blood proteins (fibrinogen for clotting, plasma proteins);
7. are a **source of energy** when carbohydrates and fats are lacking (1 g gives about 17 kJ);
8. form part of **cell membranes and cytoplasm**.

Lack of protein in the diet causes **kwashiorkor** (swollen belly, stunted growth, oedema) and marasmus.

(Any six points.)
